nt": 8.1, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.78}, "rating_margin": -15.22, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.28, "baseline_margin": -10.47, "analyst_adjustment": -0.53, "key_factors": ["District's R+8 partisan lean with incumbent Andy Harris seeking re-election", "Unanimous expert ratings from Solid R to Safe R", "Harris's large financial advantage ($1.85M cash vs. $73K for Schwartz)", "Pending Maryland redistricting litigation adds residual uncertainty"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Harris: an R+8 district, incumbency, a nine-to-one cash advantage, and every major rater calling the race Solid or Safe R. The lone July poll (R+13) and Polymarket's 90% Republican pricing reinforce that picture, so the forecast stays near the combined baseline with only slight tightening.", "watch": "Maryland's redistricting case, headed to the state Supreme Court, could redraw the seat and put Harris at risk if Democrats succeed.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 59 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Andy Harris (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Andy Harris, the incumbent Republican on Maryland's Eastern Shore, is seeking another term against Democrat Dan Schwartz in a district that leans Republican by about eight points. Harris holds a decisive fundraising edge, with $1.85 million on hand compared with roughly $73,000 for Schwartz, and outside spending in the race has been minimal. A July live-caller poll of likely voters showed Harris ahead by thirteen points.\n\nEvery major race rater, from Cook to Sabato to the Economist, places the seat in the Solid or Safe Republican column, and prediction markets price Harris near 90% to win. The main wildcard is litigation over Maryland's congressional map, where courts have so far blocked Democratic redistricting efforts but the state Supreme Court will have the final word. Absent a map change, Harris remains the clear favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1047, "